WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.990
In my last school, every teacher taught

2
00:00:02.990 --> 00:00:05.390
computing their own way, from scratch.

3
00:00:06.210 --> 00:00:08.710
Pupils met loops three times

4
00:00:08.710 --> 00:00:10.850
and functions never.

5
00:00:11.540 --> 00:00:14.440
A scheme of work fixed that in one term.

6
00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:18.020
Today: a scheme of work for computing,

7
00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:19.960
from the first grades to the last.

8
00:00:21.230 --> 00:00:23.390
A scheme of work is the plan for a

9
00:00:23.390 --> 00:00:26.450
subject across the year: which units, in

10
00:00:26.450 --> 00:00:29.970
what order, how long each takes, and how

11
00:00:29.970 --> 00:00:31.350
you’ll know pupils learned.

12
00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:34.040
It turns good lessons into a

13
00:00:34.040 --> 00:00:35.460
path pupils can climb.

14
00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:38.880
Plan the progression across grades.

15
00:00:39.700 --> 00:00:42.120
The youngest work unplugged and

16
00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:43.300
with the robot’s cards.

17
00:00:44.090 --> 00:00:46.610
Then come blocks and very short programs.

18
00:00:47.410 --> 00:00:49.750
Then real Python: variables,

19
00:00:50.210 --> 00:00:52.830
decisions, loops, lists and functions.

20
00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:55.640
Each stage builds on the last.

21
00:00:56.980 --> 00:01:00.020
For one term, plan four to six units

22
00:01:00.020 --> 00:01:02.040
of three or four lessons each.

23
00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:05.840
Give every unit one big idea, an

24
00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:08.140
unplugged start, a plugged part

25
00:01:08.140 --> 00:01:09.640
and a small project.

26
00:01:10.310 --> 00:01:12.070
Put an assessment point at the end

27
00:01:12.070 --> 00:01:14.650
of each unit: a short quiz, a

28
00:01:14.650 --> 00:01:16.430
Parsons task, or a program.

29
00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:19.720
With few devices, plan who

30
00:01:19.720 --> 00:01:20.780
gets them and when.

31
00:01:21.410 --> 00:01:23.650
A simple timetable shares the school’s

32
00:01:23.650 --> 00:01:25.790
phones or tablets between classes.

33
00:01:26.560 --> 00:01:28.940
Unplugged and paper lessons fill the

34
00:01:28.940 --> 00:01:31.300
other weeks, so no class waits a

35
00:01:31.300 --> 00:01:32.740
whole term to touch a device.

36
00:01:34.170 --> 00:01:34.570
Quick check!

37
00:01:35.190 --> 00:01:37.390
You’re planning computing for nine- and

38
00:01:37.390 --> 00:01:39.530
ten-year-olds who have never programmed.

39
00:01:40.230 --> 00:01:41.550
Which unit should come first?

40
00:01:42.820 --> 00:01:45.380
Algorithms and loops, with the robot.

41
00:01:46.170 --> 00:01:48.370
Each later unit needs the ones before

42
00:01:48.370 --> 00:01:51.310
it: you can’t teach lists before loops,

43
00:01:51.730 --> 00:01:53.670
or functions before variables.

44
00:01:54.510 --> 00:01:56.330
Draw the progression as a ladder

45
00:01:56.330 --> 00:01:58.490
before you write a single lesson.

46
00:01:59.730 --> 00:02:01.390
Make the scheme work for everyone.

47
00:02:02.170 --> 00:02:05.130
Plan paper versions of every task for

48
00:02:05.130 --> 00:02:06.790
pupils without devices at home.

49
00:02:07.410 --> 00:02:10.270
Watch that girls and quieter pupils get

50
00:02:10.270 --> 00:02:12.730
turns as driver, not only as reporter.

51
00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:15.240
And pick examples from your

52
00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:16.540
pupils’ own lives.

53
00:02:17.940 --> 00:02:21.060
Plans fail when one enthusiast leaves.

54
00:02:21.790 --> 00:02:23.090
Share the scheme in a

55
00:02:23.090 --> 00:02:24.650
folder everyone can find.

56
00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:27.380
Train one colleague each term,

57
00:02:27.700 --> 00:02:29.380
and start a computing club where

58
00:02:29.380 --> 00:02:30.380
pupils help each other.

59
00:02:30.970 --> 00:02:33.750
Then computing outlives any one teacher.

60
00:02:34.940 --> 00:02:38.120
In the app, open Sorting race, as one

61
00:02:38.120 --> 00:02:39.800
unit of your scheme might use it.

62
00:02:40.360 --> 00:02:42.580
Start unplugged with pupils sorting

63
00:02:42.580 --> 00:02:45.660
cards, then run the race on one phone:

64
00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:48.220
eight bars, then thirty-two.

65
00:02:48.810 --> 00:02:50.350
Ask the class which algorithm

66
00:02:50.350 --> 00:02:51.810
they’d choose, and why.

67
00:02:53.010 --> 00:02:53.530
Your turn.

68
00:02:54.160 --> 00:02:56.820
You lead computing, and you’re moving

69
00:02:56.820 --> 00:02:58.300
to another school next year.

70
00:02:58.830 --> 00:03:01.050
What best keeps computing going?

71
00:03:02.350 --> 00:03:04.150
Share it and train others.

72
00:03:04.990 --> 00:03:06.990
Remember: a scheme of work

73
00:03:06.990 --> 00:03:08.450
is a ladder of units.

74
00:03:09.310 --> 00:03:11.450
Progress from unplugged and the

75
00:03:11.450 --> 00:03:13.830
robot, to blocks, to Python.

76
00:03:14.520 --> 00:03:16.660
Give each unit one big idea

77
00:03:16.660 --> 00:03:18.300
and an assessment point.

78
00:03:18.980 --> 00:03:22.060
Share devices fairly, and make it last.

79
00:03:23.430 --> 00:03:25.910
Try it with your colleagues: draft one

80
00:03:25.910 --> 00:03:28.130
term of computing on a single page.

81
00:03:28.810 --> 00:03:31.950
List four to six units, the big idea of

82
00:03:31.950 --> 00:03:33.830
each, and how you’ll assess it.

83
00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:37.040
Share it, and ask one colleague

84
00:03:37.040 --> 00:03:38.420
to teach a unit with you.
